-- [Modes of Selection] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A field team tracks four adult male sparrows in a single breeding population over their entire lives. For each male they record its lifespan, its body mass at maturity, the total number of eggs it fathered, and the number of its offspring that themselves survived to reproductive age, as shown below.

| Male | Lifespan (yr) | Body mass (g) | Eggs fathered | Offspring surviving to breed |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| P | 6 | 32 | 20 | 3 |
| Q | 3 | 28 | 12 | 7 |
| R | 4 | 30 | 25 | 4 |
| S | 5 | 34 | 15 | 2 |

Assuming the population size is stable, which male has the greatest evolutionary (Darwinian) fitness?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Male P"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Male Q"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Male R"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Male S"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Evolutionary (Darwinian) fitness is not a measure of how long, large, or vigorous an individual is; OpenStax defines it as an organism''s relative contribution to the gene pool of the next generation, stating that natural selection ''selects for individuals with greater contributions to the gene pool of the next generation'' and describing fitness as ''the capacity of the members to produce more reproductively viable offspring relative to others.'' The decisive quantity is therefore the number of offspring that themselves survive to reproduce, because only descendants who reach reproductive age actually propagate an individual''s alleles forward. (Choice B) is correct: Male Q leaves 7 offspring that survive to breed, the most of any male, so it contributes the largest share of alleles to the next generation''s gene pool and has the highest fitness—even though it lived the shortest time and was the smallest. (Choice A) is incorrect: Male P has the longest lifespan, but longevity is not fitness; despite fathering many eggs, only 3 of its offspring survive to reproduce, a smaller genetic contribution than Q. (Choice C) is incorrect: Male R fathered the most eggs (25), but raw fecundity is not fitness—25 eggs yield only 4 breeding offspring, so R passes fewer alleles forward than Q. (Choice D) is incorrect: Male S is the heaviest, but a robust individual trait does not equal fitness; S leaves only 2 offspring that survive to breed, the smallest contribution to the next generation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of Darwinian fitness—relative genetic contribution to the next generation—to a data table and distinguish it from survival, body size, and raw fecundity.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '52837759-a4fc-4d66-a6c7-dfaf4121025e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '52837759-a4fc-4d66-a6c7-dfaf4121025e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('52837759-a4fc-4d66-a6c7-dfaf4121025e', 'A', 'misconception', 'fitness equals survival or longevity'),
  ('52837759-a4fc-4d66-a6c7-dfaf4121025e', 'C', 'misconception', 'fitness equals raw fecundity rather than surviving reproductive offspring'),
  ('52837759-a4fc-4d66-a6c7-dfaf4121025e', 'D', 'misconception', 'fitness equals a robust individual trait such as body size');

-- [Modes of Selection] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a bird species, males carrying an allele for extra-long, brightly colored tail feathers are spotted and killed by hawks far more often than short-tailed males, so long-tailed males have a noticeably shorter average lifespan. However, females strongly prefer long-tailed males, and each breeding season a long-tailed male fathers several times as many surviving chicks as a short-tailed male does. Over many generations, what is the most likely fate of the long-tail allele in this population?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "It increases in frequency, because long-tailed males contribute more offspring to the next generation''s gene pool."}, {"label": "B", "text": "It decreases in frequency, because long-tailed males have a shorter average lifespan than short-tailed males."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It stays roughly constant, because the mating advantage and the survival disadvantage offset each other."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It increases in frequency, because long-tailed males survive to breed across more seasons than short-tailed males."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Evolutionary (Darwinian) fitness is not an organism''s own survival or longevity; OpenStax states that natural selection "selects for individuals with greater contributions to the gene pool of the next generation," and that a trait can be favored even when it is "detrimental to the individual''s survival" as long as it raises reproductive success. Because the allele''s fate is set by differential reproduction, you must weigh how many offspring each type of male leaves, not how long each male lives. (Choice A) is correct: long-tailed males father several times as many surviving chicks per season, so a larger share of the next generation carries the long-tail allele, and its frequency rises over time — the essence of selection by differential reproduction and increased representation in the gene pool. (Choice B) is incorrect: it equates fitness with survival/lifespan, but evolutionary success is measured by reproductive contribution, and here the strong reproductive advantage outweighs the survival cost — the same reason sexual selection can favor survival-reducing ornaments. (Choice C) is incorrect: it assumes opposing pressures automatically cancel to a stable frequency, but the two effects are not equal — the reproductive advantage is described as several-fold per season and dominates, so the allele does not stay constant. (Choice D) is incorrect: it reaches the right direction for the wrong reason, crediting the increase to greater longevity, yet long-tailed males actually die younger; their advantage comes from per-season mating success, not from breeding over more seasons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fitness concept — that selection acts through differential reproductive contribution to the next generation''s gene pool rather than through individual survival — to predict allele-frequency change in a novel scenario.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '28f9b4dd-112f-4bd2-9265-6410bfe935b7';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '28f9b4dd-112f-4bd2-9265-6410bfe935b7';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('28f9b4dd-112f-4bd2-9265-6410bfe935b7', 'B', 'misconception', 'fitness_equals_survival_or_longevity'),
  ('28f9b4dd-112f-4bd2-9265-6410bfe935b7', 'C', 'misconception', 'opposing_pressures_must_cancel_to_equilibrium'),
  ('28f9b4dd-112f-4bd2-9265-6410bfe935b7', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'right_direction_wrong_mechanism_longevity');

-- [Punnett Squares & Monohybrid Crosses] ans=D hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In peas, yellow seed color \((Y)\) is completely dominant to green \((y)\). Two heterozygous plants \((Yy)\) are crossed, producing a large F2 generation. A researcher discards all green-seeded offspring and then picks one yellow-seeded plant at random. If that plant is self-crossed, its own offspring may or may not include green seeds. What is the probability that the randomly chosen yellow-seeded plant is heterozygous \((Yy)\)?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\tfrac{1}{3}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\tfrac{2}{3}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' A \(Yy \times Yy\) cross yields a genotypic ratio of \(1\ YY : 2\ Yy : 1\ yy\) across four equally likely Punnett-square boxes. Because green-seeded (\(yy\)) plants are discarded, the sample is restricted to the yellow-seeded offspring only, so the probability must be conditioned on that smaller group rather than read off the full square. Among the yellow plants the genotypes occur as \(1\ YY : 2\ Yy\), a total of three equally likely yellow individuals; the textbook confirms this experimentally, noting that when Mendel self-crossed the yellow F2 plants, "one-third of the plants bred true, and two-thirds of the plants segregated at a 3:1 ratio," so the true-breeding plants "had homozygous (YY) genotypes." (Choice A) is incorrect: \(\tfrac{1}{4}\) is the unconditional probability of \(YY\) (or of \(yy\)) taken straight from the four-box square, ignoring that the green plants were removed and that two boxes are heterozygous. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\tfrac{1}{3}\) reverses the ratio, giving the fraction of yellow plants that are true-breeding homozygotes (\(YY\)) rather than the fraction that are heterozygotes. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) is the unconditional probability of \(Yy\) in the full \(1{:}2{:}1\) population and would be correct only if no offspring had been discarded; conditioning on yellow raises this value. (Choice D) is correct: restricting to yellow seeds leaves \(1\ YY : 2\ Yy\), so \(P(Yy \mid \text{yellow}) = \tfrac{2}{2+1} = \tfrac{2}{3}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply Mendelian ratios and conditional probability to a filtered subpopulation rather than recall a fixed genotypic ratio.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '05ba32ba-9a5d-4c0e-bc05-605bb82f4ec0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '05ba32ba-9a5d-4c0e-bc05-605bb82f4ec0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('05ba32ba-9a5d-4c0e-bc05-605bb82f4ec0', 'A', 'misconception', 'reads single genotype probability off the raw Punnett square without conditioning'),
  ('05ba32ba-9a5d-4c0e-bc05-605bb82f4ec0', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'conditions correctly but reports the homozygous (true-breeding) fraction instead of the heterozygous fraction'),
  ('05ba32ba-9a5d-4c0e-bc05-605bb82f4ec0', 'C', 'misconception', 'gives the unconditional P(Yy) and ignores that green offspring were discarded');

-- [Punnett Squares & Monohybrid Crosses] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In pea plants, yellow seed color \(Y\) is completely dominant to green \(y\). A breeder wants to determine the genotype of a yellow-seeded plant of unknown genotype, so she performs a test cross by mating it with a green-seeded plant. The unknown plant is in fact heterozygous. If the cross yields three seeds, what is the probability that all three seeds are yellow?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\tfrac{3}{8}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\tfrac{1}{8}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' In a test cross, a dominant-expressing organism is crossed with a homozygous recessive individual; when the unknown parent is heterozygous (\(Yy \times yy\)), the offspring "exhibit a 1:1 ratio of heterozygotes and recessive homozygotes" (OpenStax Biology 2e, line 14845), so each seed independently has a \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) chance of being yellow \(Yy\) and a \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) chance of being green \(yy\). Because fertilization events are independent, "the probability of two independent events occurring together can be calculated by multiplying the individual probabilities of each event occurring alone" (the product rule, lines 14603-14604). (Choice A) is incorrect: \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) is the probability that a single seed is yellow and ignores that the outcome for all three seeds must be combined; it fails to compound the independent events. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\tfrac{3}{8}\) results from multiplying the single-seed probability by three (\(3 \times \tfrac{1}{8}\)) or misapplying the sum rule, treating three independent "and" events as though they were additive rather than multiplicative. (Choice C) is correct: applying the product rule to three independent seeds each with probability \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) of being yellow gives \(\tfrac{1}{2} \times \tfrac{1}{2} \times \tfrac{1}{2} = \tfrac{1}{8}\). (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\tfrac{1}{4}\) is \(\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)^2\), the probability that only two seeds (not all three) are yellow, or it reflects confusing the \(1{:}1\) test-cross ratio with the \(\tfrac{1}{4}\) homozygous-recessive frequency of a heterozygote self-cross. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the test-cross \(1{:}1\) ratio together with the product rule for independent fertilization events to compute a compound probability the stem does not hand you directly.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '7eb69fda-3956-4a77-aeb6-90bb9a06af93';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7eb69fda-3956-4a77-aeb6-90bb9a06af93';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7eb69fda-3956-4a77-aeb6-90bb9a06af93', 'A', 'misconception', 'single-event probability, forgets to compound independent events'),
  ('7eb69fda-3956-4a77-aeb6-90bb9a06af93', 'B', 'misconception', 'applies sum rule instead of product rule (additive not multiplicative)'),
  ('7eb69fda-3956-4a77-aeb6-90bb9a06af93', 'D', 'misconception', 'compounds only two of three events, or confuses test-cross ratio with self-cross yy frequency');

-- [Mutations] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A geneticist sequencing the β-globin coding region in two unrelated healthy people finds that their sequences differ at exactly one nucleotide, yet both individuals produce β-globin protein with an identical, fully functional amino acid sequence. Which single-nucleotide difference is most consistent with this observation?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A substitution at the third position of a codon"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A substitution at the first position of a codon"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A deletion of one nucleotide within a codon"}, {"label": "D", "text": "An insertion of one nucleotide within a codon"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The genetic code is degenerate, meaning a given amino acid can be specified by more than one codon; the textbook notes that codons specifying the same amino acid "typically only differ by one nucleotide," and that difference is usually at the third base of the codon. To keep the protein sequence identical and fully functional, the single-nucleotide difference must not change any amino acid or shift the reading frame. (Choice A) is correct: silent mutations "are usually due to a substitution in the third base of a codon, which often represents the same amino acid as the original codon," so a third-position substitution is the change most likely to leave the amino acid sequence unchanged and the protein fully functional. (Choice B) is incorrect: a substitution at the first position of a codon usually specifies a different amino acid (a missense change) because degeneracy rarely tolerates first-base changes, so the protein sequence would most likely be altered rather than identical. (Choice C) is incorrect: deleting one nucleotide alters the translational reading frame downstream (a frameshift), and "the resultant protein is usually nonfunctional," which contradicts the observed identical, functional protein. (Choice D) is incorrect: inserting one nucleotide likewise shifts the reading frame for every subsequent codon, changing the message for the rest of the protein and typically producing a nonfunctional product. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the degeneracy of the genetic code and the consequences of substitutions versus insertions/deletions to infer which single-nucleotide change preserves an identical, functional protein.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f7a4ba20-2038-4339-b36b-1dfadf4be9f8';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f7a4ba20-2038-4339-b36b-1dfadf4be9f8';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f7a4ba20-2038-4339-b36b-1dfadf4be9f8', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong codon position for silent change'),
  ('f7a4ba20-2038-4339-b36b-1dfadf4be9f8', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'frameshift confused with silent substitution'),
  ('f7a4ba20-2038-4339-b36b-1dfadf4be9f8', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'frameshift confused with silent substitution');

-- [Evolutionary Patterns] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A population of 5,000 leaf beetles lives along a riverbank. Two alleles at a color locus occur at equal frequency: the green allele at 0.50 and the brown allele at 0.50. A sudden flash flood drowns roughly 95% of the beetles, leaving only about 250 survivors. The flood swept away individuals indiscriminately, sparing whichever beetles happened to be on higher ground when the water rose. Among the survivors, the green allele frequency is now 0.80 and the brown allele frequency is 0.20. Which conclusion about this shift in allele frequencies is best supported?

',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The change reflects chance sampling of the survivors rather than a survival advantage of the green allele."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The change shows that green beetles were better able to survive the flood, an example of natural selection."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The change occurred because the survivors migrated away and founded a new population unlike the original."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The change means the surviving population now holds greater genetic variation than the pre-flood population."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' A natural catastrophe that kills a large fraction of a population at random magnifies genetic drift, a phenomenon the textbook calls the bottleneck effect; the survivors'' genetic structure becomes the whole population''s structure and may differ substantially from the pre-disaster population. Because the flood swept beetles away indiscriminately, the surviving allele frequencies are simply a chance sample, not the result of any color-based advantage — the textbook stresses that in genetic drift some individuals leave more offspring "not due to an advantage conferred by some genetically-encoded trait, but just because" of luck. (Choice A) is correct: with survival determined by where a beetle happened to be rather than by its color, the jump in the green allele from 0.50 to 0.80 is chance sampling (a bottleneck-driven episode of genetic drift), not evidence that green conferred a fitness advantage. (Choice B) is incorrect: natural selection requires differential reproduction tied to a heritable trait, but the stem states the flood killed beetles indiscriminately, so a rise in green allele frequency does not indicate that green beetles survived better — assuming any frequency change equals selection is a common error. (Choice C) is incorrect: this describes the founder effect, which occurs when a small group leaves to start a new population in a new location; here the survivors remain in place after a catastrophe reduces the existing population, which is a bottleneck, not a founder event. (Choice D) is incorrect: a bottleneck suddenly wipes out much of the gene pool and therefore reduces genetic variation, and small surviving populations are more susceptible to further drift, so variation decreases rather than increases. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of genetic drift and the bottleneck effect to allele-frequency data and distinguish chance-driven change from fitness-driven selection.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '509fc911-18a8-47b0-b4b2-33a75fb04592';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '509fc911-18a8-47b0-b4b2-33a75fb04592';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('509fc911-18a8-47b0-b4b2-33a75fb04592', 'B', 'misconception', 'any allele-frequency change means natural selection'),
  ('509fc911-18a8-47b0-b4b2-33a75fb04592', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'confusing the bottleneck effect with the founder effect'),
  ('509fc911-18a8-47b0-b4b2-33a75fb04592', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'believing a bottleneck raises rather than lowers genetic variation');

-- [Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A large mainland population of beetles has been in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium for many generations, with shell-color allele frequencies \(p\) and \(q\) holding steady. During a storm, roughly a dozen beetles are blown onto a distant, previously uninhabited island, where they establish a small isolated colony that no other beetles reach. Assuming shell color has no effect on survival or mating on the island, which force is most likely to drive the island colony''s allele frequencies away from the mainland values in the next few generations?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Genetic drift, because chance sampling has a large effect in a small population"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Natural selection, because the favored shell-color allele rises in frequency"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Gene flow, because migrating beetles keep introducing mainland alleles"}, {"label": "D", "text": "New mutation, because a novel shell-color allele arises in the colony"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The Hardy-Weinberg principle assumes ''an infinitely large population and conditions with no mutations, migration, emigration, or selective pressure for or against genotype'' (OpenStax Biology 2e, line 22819); when a condition fails, the population evolves and \(p\) and \(q\) shift. The scenario removes the large-population assumption (a dozen founders) while explicitly holding the others: shell color is selectively neutral, the colony is isolated, and no new allele is stated to appear. You must match the specific violated assumption to its corresponding force. (Choice A) is correct: the founders are a tiny, unrepresentative sample of the mainland gene pool, and the text states that ''small populations are more susceptible to the forces of genetic drift'' while ''large populations... are buffered against the effects of chance'' (lines 22985-22986); a founding event by a small unlikely-representative group is the founder effect, a form of drift that shifts allele frequencies purely by chance (lines 23010-23013). (Choice B) is incorrect: natural selection requires ''selective pressure for or against genotype'' (line 22819), but the stem specifies shell color has no effect on survival or mating, so no allele is favored and selection cannot be the driver. (Choice C) is incorrect: gene flow is ''the flow of alleles in and out of a population due to the migration of individuals'' (line 23071), yet the colony is isolated and no other beetles reach it, so there is no ongoing migration to introduce mainland alleles. (Choice D) is incorrect: a new mutation introduces novel variation (lines 23085-23087), but a specific mutation arising is a rare, unstated event and would change one allele at a time; nothing in the stem indicates a novel allele appeared, so it is far less likely than chance sampling of the existing alleles to reshape frequencies in just a few generations. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Hardy-Weinberg assumptions to a novel founding scenario and determine which single assumption was violated and thus which evolutionary force dominates.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '011d2b2c-13af-474c-a0f9-9c3ae920ec3c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '011d2b2c-13af-474c-a0f9-9c3ae920ec3c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('011d2b2c-13af-474c-a0f9-9c3ae920ec3c', 'B', 'misconception', 'default-to-natural-selection'),
  ('011d2b2c-13af-474c-a0f9-9c3ae920ec3c', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'force-contradicted-by-stem'),
  ('011d2b2c-13af-474c-a0f9-9c3ae920ec3c', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'unstated-mechanism');

-- [Evolutionary Patterns] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a population of ground squirrels, a field team follows four adult females for life and records the data below. All four begin adulthood in the same breeding season, and juveniles are counted as reaching reproductive age only if they survive to breed themselves.

- Squirrel W: largest body mass; lived 6 years; bore 20 pups, of which 3 survived to reproduce.
- Squirrel X: average body mass; lived 3 years; bore 8 pups, of which 7 survived to reproduce.
- Squirrel Y: smallest body mass; lived 7 years (longest-lived); bore 12 pups, of which 4 survived to reproduce.
- Squirrel Z: average body mass; lived 4 years; bore 15 pups, of which 5 survived to reproduce.

Which squirrel has the greatest evolutionary (Darwinian) fitness?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Squirrel W, because she had the largest body mass and bore the most pups"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Squirrel X, because she left the most offspring that survived to reproduce"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Squirrel Y, because she had the longest lifespan of the four"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Squirrel Z, because she had an average body mass and bore many pups"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Evolutionary (Darwinian) fitness is defined by an individual''s contribution to the gene pool of the next generation: natural selection "selects for individuals with greater contributions to the gene pool of the next generation" (lines 23154-23156). What counts is not how large an individual is, how long it lives, or even how many offspring it bears, but how many of those offspring themselves survive to reproduce, because a phenotype that never reaches the next breeding generation "will not pass to the next generation" (lines 23152-23155). Comparing only the number of pups that survived to reproduce, the values are W = 3, X = 7, Y = 4, and Z = 5. (Choice A) is incorrect: large body mass and a high raw birth count do not equal fitness, and W''s 20 pups yielded only 3 that reproduced; traits unrelated to reproductive contribution, and even traits detrimental to survival, do not raise fitness (lines 23291-23295). (Choice B) is correct: with 7 pups surviving to reproduce, X made the largest contribution to the next generation''s gene pool despite an average body size and the shortest lifespan, which is precisely how fitness is measured (lines 23155-23161). (Choice C) is incorrect: longevity by itself is not fitness; Y lived longest but only 4 of her pups reproduced, so she contributed fewer descendants to the gene pool than X (lines 23155-23156). (Choice D) is incorrect: an average build and many pups born do not settle fitness, because only 5 of Z''s pups survived to reproduce, fewer than X''s 7 (lines 23158-23161). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of evolutionary fitness to novel demographic data and identify contribution to the next generation, rather than body size or lifespan, as the operative measure.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '391acf9a-2e2d-4c2c-a0c7-1085c4f901e7';
