  ('391acf9a-2e2d-4c2c-a0c7-1085c4f901e7', 'A', 'misconception', 'bigger/stronger organism is the fittest'),
  ('391acf9a-2e2d-4c2c-a0c7-1085c4f901e7', 'C', 'misconception', 'longer lifespan equals higher fitness'),
  ('391acf9a-2e2d-4c2c-a0c7-1085c4f901e7', 'D', 'misconception', 'raw number of offspring born equals fitness');

-- [Speciation & Isolation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A conservation team manages a small, isolated herd of an endangered antelope. Because so few founders remain, individuals are increasingly mated with close relatives across generations. Over time, the team observes a rising incidence of a metabolic disorder caused by a rare recessive allele that was already present at a low frequency in the founding animals. Which change in the herd best explains why this disorder is now appearing more often?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The proportion of individuals homozygous for the recessive allele has increased in the herd."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The frequency of the recessive allele in the herd''s gene pool has increased."}, {"label": "C", "text": "New copies of the deleterious allele have been introduced into the herd by mutation."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The overall heterozygosity of the herd has increased across its genetic loci."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Inbreeding, the mating of closely related individuals, does not change how common an allele is in the gene pool; instead, it changes how alleles are combined into genotypes, bringing together deleterious recessive alleles more often. The textbook explains that a rare recessive disease allele stays hidden in carriers and manifests only when an individual inherits two copies, and that when relatives interbreed this "dramatically increase[s] the likelihood of two carriers mating and eventually producing diseased offspring," a phenomenon called inbreeding depression (lines 22930-22939). (Choice A) is correct: inbreeding raises the fraction of homozygous genotypes, so recessive alleles that were previously masked in heterozygous carriers are now expressed in homozygous recessive individuals, which is exactly why the disorder appears more frequently. (Choice B) is incorrect: inbreeding rearranges genotypes without adding or removing alleles, so the allele frequency in the gene pool stays essentially the same, as the text notes when it says the rare allele "maintains itself at low levels in the gene pool" (lines 22936-22937). (Choice C) is incorrect: new copies of an allele arise through mutation, a separate evolutionary force; inbreeding only changes the pairing of alleles already present in the founders, not the DNA sequence. (Choice D) is incorrect: inbreeding lowers heterozygosity by producing more homozygotes, which is why conservation breeders deliberately try to increase genetic variability to reduce the risks of inbreeding (lines 22928-22930). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of inbreeding to a conservation scenario and distinguish a change in genotype frequency from changes in allele frequency, mutation, and heterozygosity.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3b5c160a-3341-46ad-960c-910f8fc5db67';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3b5c160a-3341-46ad-960c-910f8fc5db67';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3b5c160a-3341-46ad-960c-910f8fc5db67', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing genotype-frequency change with allele-frequency change'),
  ('3b5c160a-3341-46ad-960c-910f8fc5db67', 'C', 'misconception', 'attributing inbreeding effects to new mutation'),
  ('3b5c160a-3341-46ad-960c-910f8fc5db67', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'inbreeding raises rather than lowers heterozygosity');

-- [Dominance Patterns] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A plant breeder crosses a true-breeding variety with solid crimson petals against a true-breeding variety with solid ivory petals. All of the F1 offspring are identical: each petal bears sharply bordered crimson and ivory patches side by side, with no crimson pigment appearing in the ivory patches and no ivory regions within the crimson patches. When the F1 plants are self-crossed, the F2 generation appears in an approximate 1 crimson : 2 patched : 1 ivory ratio. Which pattern of inheritance best accounts for the F1 petal phenotype?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Codominance"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Incomplete dominance"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Complete dominance"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Epistasis"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Distinguishing among dominance patterns requires interpreting the heterozygote phenotype: codominance means both alleles are simultaneously and fully expressed in the heterozygote, so both parental products appear distinctly, whereas incomplete dominance produces a single intermediate phenotype that blends the two homozygous states. Here the F1 petals show sharply bordered crimson and ivory patches with each pigment expressed at full intensity in its own region — both parental phenotypes are present simultaneously and distinctly, not blended. (Choice A) is correct: the heterozygote independently displays both the crimson allele''s product and the ivory allele''s product in the same tissue with neither masking or diluting the other, which is the defining signature of codominance; the accompanying \(1{:}2{:}1\) ratio reflects the standard Mendelian monohybrid genotypic ratio that both codominant and incompletely dominant self-crosses produce, so the ratio alone cannot distinguish the two. (Choice B) is incorrect: incomplete dominance would yield a uniform intermediate phenotype in the heterozygote — for example, pink petals from a crimson \(\times\) ivory cross, analogous to the pink snapdragon — rather than discrete regions of full crimson and full ivory. (Choice C) is incorrect: under complete dominance one allele would mask the other and the F1 would resemble a single homozygous parent (all crimson or all ivory), which contradicts the observed two-color F1. (Choice D) is incorrect: epistasis is an interaction between alleles of two different genes in which one gene masks the phenotypic effect of another, whereas this cross involves two alleles of a single gene, so it cannot explain the F1 phenotype. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the dominance patterns to a described heterozygote phenotype and infer which mechanism produces two distinct, simultaneously expressed traits rather than a blend.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing codominance with incomplete dominance'),
  ('d383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'default assumption of simple dominance'),
  ('d383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'two-gene interaction applied to a single-gene case');

-- [Gene Mapping & Recombination] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A geneticist maps three linked genes on one Drosophila chromosome by performing separate two-point testcrosses for each gene pair and recording the recombination frequencies below.

| Gene pair | Recombination frequency |
|-----------|-------------------------|
| \(A\)–\(B\) | \(12\%\) |
| \(B\)–\(C\) | \(15\%\) |
| \(A\)–\(C\) | \(23\%\) |

Assuming the genes are arranged serially along the chromosome, which value is the best estimate of the map distance between the two outermost genes?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(27\\ \\text{cM}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(23\\ \\text{cM}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(15\\ \\text{cM}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(3\\ \\text{cM}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The textbook explains that map distances in centimorgans correspond to recombination frequencies in percent and that this linear representation "allowed Sturtevant to additively calculate distances between several genes on the same chromosome." A key consequence of additivity is that a directly measured two-point frequency between two far-apart genes underestimates their true separation, because a double crossover between those genes returns the outer alleles to the parental arrangement and goes undetected; summing the two shorter, adjacent intervals recovers those hidden events. Here the largest measured frequency, \(A\)–\(C = 23\%\), identifies \(A\) and \(C\) as the outermost genes with \(B\) in the middle, giving the order \(A\)–\(B\)–\(C\). (Choice A) is correct: adding the two adjacent intervals, \(12\ \text{cM} + 15\ \text{cM} = 27\ \text{cM}\), gives the most accurate estimate of the outer distance because it captures double-crossover events the single \(A\)–\(C\) testcross misses. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(23\ \text{cM}\) simply reads the directly measured \(A\)–\(C\) frequency from the table, which is an underestimate of the true distance for the very reason that double crossovers between distant markers are not scored as recombinant. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(15\ \text{cM}\) is only the single largest interval (\(B\)–\(C\)) and omits the adjacent \(A\)–\(B\) interval needed to span from \(A\) to \(C\). (Choice D) is incorrect: \(3\ \text{cM}\) subtracts the two intervals (\(15 - 12\)), which would place the genes overlapping rather than end to end and contradicts the additivity of map distances. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the additivity of map distances and the effect of undetected double crossovers to recombination data to deduce gene order and select the most accurate outer distance.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '1bb7d148-1c00-4026-aa69-2bb0a3f12c62';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '1bb7d148-1c00-4026-aa69-2bb0a3f12c62';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('1bb7d148-1c00-4026-aa69-2bb0a3f12c62', 'B', 'misconception', 'reads the directly measured two-point frequency and ignores undetected double crossovers'),
  ('1bb7d148-1c00-4026-aa69-2bb0a3f12c62', 'C', 'misconception', 'uses only the single largest interval instead of summing adjacent intervals'),
  ('1bb7d148-1c00-4026-aa69-2bb0a3f12c62', 'D', 'misconception', 'subtracts adjacent intervals instead of adding them');

-- [Evolutionary Patterns] ans=B hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A biologist tracks four males in a population of a long-lived lizard over their entire lifetimes and records the data below:

- Male W: survives 8 years (the longest of the group), grows to large body size, and leaves 2 offspring that reach reproductive age.
- Male X: survives 3 years, grows to average body size, and leaves 9 offspring that reach reproductive age.
- Male Y: survives 5 years, grows to the largest body size and wins most territorial contests, and leaves 4 offspring that reach reproductive age.
- Male Z: survives 6 years, grows to small body size, and leaves 3 offspring that reach reproductive age.

Assuming all offspring are equally viable, which male has the greatest evolutionary (Darwinian) fitness?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Male W, because it survived the longest of the four males"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Male X, because it left the most offspring that reached reproductive age"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Male Y, because it reached the largest body size and won the most contests"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Male Z, because it balanced a long lifespan with a moderate number of offspring"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Evolutionary (Darwinian) fitness measures how much an individual contributes alleles to the gene pool of the next generation; OpenStax states that natural selection "selects for individuals with greater contributions to the gene pool of the next generation" and defines evolutionary fitness as an "individual''s ability to survive and reproduce." Because evolution results from "differential reproduction of individuals," the relevant currency is the number of offspring that themselves survive to reproduce, not longevity, body size, or dominance. (Choice A) is incorrect because a long lifespan raises fitness only insofar as it yields more surviving offspring; Male W lived the longest yet left just 2 offspring, so its genetic contribution to the next generation is small. (Choice B) is correct: Male X left 9 offspring that reached reproductive age, the largest contribution to the next generation''s gene pool, which is exactly what evolutionary fitness measures even though it had the shortest lifespan and only average size. (Choice C) is incorrect because large body size and winning territorial contests are traits that may help an organism acquire mates, but fitness is assessed by realized reproductive output; despite dominating contests, Male Y left only 4 surviving offspring. (Choice D) is incorrect because "balancing" lifespan and offspring number is not how fitness is defined; Male Z left just 3 surviving offspring, fewer than Male X, so its contribution to the gene pool is lower regardless of its longevity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of evolutionary fitness to lifetime reproductive data and recognize that surviving offspring, not survival time or body size, determine an individual''s genetic contribution to the next generation.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a801b4c4-4903-48ae-9529-f10e3cee9fb0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a801b4c4-4903-48ae-9529-f10e3cee9fb0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a801b4c4-4903-48ae-9529-f10e3cee9fb0', 'A', 'misconception', 'fitness equals longevity/survival'),
  ('a801b4c4-4903-48ae-9529-f10e3cee9fb0', 'C', 'misconception', 'fitness equals size/strength/dominance'),
  ('a801b4c4-4903-48ae-9529-f10e3cee9fb0', 'D', 'misconception', 'fitness is a balance of lifespan and offspring');

-- [Dominance Patterns] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a species of songbird, feather pigment is controlled by a single locus with two alleles. Birds homozygous for one allele produce only a yellow pigment protein in their feathers; birds homozygous for the other allele produce only a black pigment protein. When the two homozygous types are crossed, biochemical assay of the F1 feathers detects both the yellow pigment protein and the black pigment protein, each at the same amount found in the respective homozygous parent, and the feathers appear boldly striped in yellow and black. Which inheritance pattern best accounts for the F1 phenotype?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Codominance of the two pigment alleles"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Incomplete dominance of the two pigment alleles"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Complete dominance of the black allele"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A dosage effect of a single pigment allele"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Extensions to Mendelian inheritance are distinguished by how the heterozygote expresses its two alleles: codominance is the simultaneous, full expression of both alleles, whereas incomplete dominance produces a single intermediate phenotype that resembles neither homozygous parent. The key data here are that the heterozygote makes both parental gene products, each at its full homozygous amount, and displays both colors as distinct stripes. (Choice A) is correct: because the heterozygote expresses both the yellow and the black pigment proteins simultaneously and fully—so both parental traits appear together rather than blending—this is the defining signature of codominance, exactly analogous to a heterozygote for the MN blood group expressing both the M and N antigens on its red blood cells. (Choice B) is incorrect: incomplete dominance would yield a single intermediate phenotype (for example, uniformly olive or dull green feathers) reflecting a blended or reduced output, not the full, separate expression of both distinct pigment proteins detected here. (Choice C) is incorrect: complete dominance of the black allele would mask the yellow allele entirely, so the heterozygote would appear solid black and no yellow pigment protein would be detectable, contradicting the assay result. (Choice D) is incorrect: a dosage effect would leave one product expressed at a reduced level in the heterozygote, but the assay shows each protein at its full homozygous amount, so no allele is being under-expressed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of codominance and incomplete dominance to biochemical and phenotypic evidence about the heterozygote in order to infer the underlying inheritance pattern.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd4d44718-4579-4f88-bb34-2de9641930f9';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd4d44718-4579-4f88-bb34-2de9641930f9';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d4d44718-4579-4f88-bb34-2de9641930f9', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing codominance with incomplete dominance'),
  ('d4d44718-4579-4f88-bb34-2de9641930f9', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'defaulting to simple Mendelian dominance'),
  ('d4d44718-4579-4f88-bb34-2de9641930f9', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'invoking dosage/gene-quantity effect not supported by the data');

-- [Natural Selection & Fitness] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a meadow population of wildflowers, taller plants reliably set more seeds than shorter plants, and this height advantage holds every season. Curious why average height never climbs across generations, a researcher collects seeds separately from the tallest and shortest plants and raises both seed groups together in a uniform greenhouse. The two groups grow to the same average height, and that height differs from either parent group depending only on the fertilizer dose the greenhouse receives. Given this result, the most direct reason natural selection fails to drive height evolution in the meadow is that the height differences among the wild plants',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "provide no reproductive advantage to the plants"}, {"label": "B", "text": "appeared only after the soil conditions began changing"}, {"label": "C", "text": "are not passed from parent plants to their offspring"}, {"label": "D", "text": "exist in a population too small for selection to act"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Natural selection changes a population across generations only when the phenotypic variation it acts on is heritable: OpenStax states that differences among individuals "must have some genetic basis; otherwise, the selection will not lead to change in the next generation," and gives the exact example of variation in "an individual''s height because of better nutrition rather than different genes" (Biology 2e, lines 21762-21765, 22906-22911). The greenhouse experiment is the test for heritability—raising offspring of tall and short parents together removes environmental differences, and here the offspring show no trace of their parents'' height, tracking fertilizer instead, so the wild height differences do not transmit to the next generation. (Choice A) is incorrect: the stem states taller plants reliably set more seeds every season, so the trait clearly does confer a reproductive advantage; the barrier is that this advantage cannot accumulate, not that it is absent, and selection acts on existing variation whether or not every variant is favored (lines 21762-21763). (Choice B) is incorrect: the variation natural selection works on "is already in a population and does not arise in response to an environmental change," as with antibiotic-resistance alleles that predate the antibiotic (lines 21998-22005); the timing of the differences is not what the experiment reveals, and it does not explain the failure to evolve. (Choice C) is correct: because the seeds of tall and short parents grow to the same height under common conditions, the wild differences are not inherited, and selection cannot shift a trait distribution it cannot transmit (lines 21762-21765, 22906-22911). (Choice D) is incorrect: small population size accelerates random genetic drift, a separate non-selective force, and would not make a genetically based advantageous trait fail to respond to selection; nothing in the data speaks to population size (lines 22807-22811). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that selection requires heritable variation by interpreting a common-garden breeding result—offspring resembling their fertilizer rather than their parents—to conclude that the trait differences are nongenetic and therefore cannot evolve.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b2bdc0b7-4dba-4d69-8a9a-532f8058dcff';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b2bdc0b7-4dba-4d69-8a9a-532f8058dcff';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b2bdc0b7-4dba-4d69-8a9a-532f8058dcff', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses missing fitness advantage with missing heritability'),
  ('b2bdc0b7-4dba-4d69-8a9a-532f8058dcff', 'B', 'misconception', 'Lamarckian belief that variation arises in response to the environment'),
  ('b2bdc0b7-4dba-4d69-8a9a-532f8058dcff', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'invokes genetic drift / small-population effects unsupported by the data');

-- [Punnett Squares & Monohybrid Crosses] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In pea plants, round seeds \((R)\) are completely dominant to wrinkled seeds \((r)\). A breeder has a round-seeded plant of unknown genotype and wants to determine that genotype. She crosses it with a wrinkled-seeded plant and scores a large number of offspring, obtaining approximately \(50\%\) round-seeded and \(50\%\) wrinkled-seeded plants. Which conclusion is best supported by these results?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The round-seeded parent is homozygous dominant \\((RR)\\)."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The round-seeded parent is heterozygous \\((Rr)\\)."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The wrinkled-seeded parent carries one dominant \\(R\\) allele."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Round and wrinkled show incomplete dominance in this cross."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Crossing an organism that shows the dominant trait with a homozygous recessive individual is a test cross, used to reveal whether the dominant-expressing parent is homozygous or heterozygous. The wrinkled parent is \(rr\) and can donate only an \(r\) allele, so every offspring''s phenotype is determined by the allele it receives from the round-seeded parent. (Choice B) is correct: because roughly half the offspring are wrinkled \((rr)\), the round parent must have supplied an \(r\) allele to about half its gametes and an \(R\) allele to the other half; the textbook states that in a test cross, \"if the dominant expressing organism is a heterozygote, the F1 offspring will exhibit a 1:1 ratio of heterozygotes and recessive homozygotes,\" so the round parent is \(Rr\). (Choice A) is incorrect: a homozygous dominant \((RR)\) parent could donate only \(R\) alleles, so a test cross would yield all round-seeded offspring and no wrinkled ones ("all F1 offspring will be heterozygotes expressing the dominant trait"); the appearance of wrinkled offspring rules this out. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the logic by attributing the recessive offspring to the wrong parent — a wrinkled-seeded plant is by definition homozygous recessive \((rr)\) and cannot carry a hidden \(R\), so the wrinkled offspring trace to a recessive allele from the round parent, not from the wrinkled parent. (Choice D) is incorrect: incomplete dominance would produce heterozygotes with an intermediate phenotype rather than two distinct classes, and the stem specifies complete dominance; the clean split into fully round and fully wrinkled offspring is exactly the Mendelian test-cross outcome, not blending. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the logic of the test cross and the law of segregation to reason backward from an observed offspring ratio to the unknown parental genotype.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c899414a-ca16-43c2-b6ca-a636f802c88b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c899414a-ca16-43c2-b6ca-a636f802c88b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c899414a-ca16-43c2-b6ca-a636f802c88b', 'A', 'misconception', 'dominant phenotype means homozygous dominant genotype'),
  ('c899414a-ca16-43c2-b6ca-a636f802c88b', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'attributing recessive offspring to the recessive parent instead of the dominant parent'),
  ('c899414a-ca16-43c2-b6ca-a636f802c88b', 'D', 'misconception', 'reading a 1:1 phenotypic split as evidence of incomplete dominance');

-- [Mendelian Foundations] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a fruit fly population, a newly arisen allele produces a leg-on-head phenotype, and this phenotype appears in every heterozygote that carries one copy of the new allele alongside one copy of the standard-form allele. The standard form is designated the wild type. Which statement about the wild-type allele in this population must be true?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "It is dominant to the new variant allele"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It is more prevalent than the new variant allele"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It is the most recently arisen allele at the locus"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It is expressed only in homozygous individuals"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' By convention, the wild type is the most common, standard phenotype or genotype in a population, and all rarer forms are treated as variants that deviate from it; critically, the textbook states that a variant "may be recessive or dominant to the wild-type allele," so the wild-type label is set by frequency, not by any dominance relationship (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 14937-14940). (Choice A) is incorrect: students often assume the wild type is always dominant, but here the new allele is expressed in every heterozygote that also carries the standard allele, which shows the variant is dominant to the wild type (as in the Antennapedia mutation), so the wild-type allele is actually recessive in this cross. (Choice B) is correct: the standard form is called the wild type precisely because it is the most common form in the population, so the wild-type allele must be more prevalent than the newly arisen variant. (Choice C) is incorrect: the wild type is the established standard, whereas a newly arisen allele is by definition a variant, so the wild-type allele is the older, not the more recent, allele at the locus. (Choice D) is incorrect: an allele can be expressed in heterozygotes as well as homozygotes depending on dominance, and being wild type does not restrict its expression to homozygotes; the standard form is typically visible across many genotypes because it is common. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of the wild type as a frequency-based standard to a scenario in which the variant, not the wild type, behaves dominantly.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8a6e350d-9a0e-4f2a-b51d-bbafbb436e81';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8a6e350d-9a0e-4f2a-b51d-bbafbb436e81';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('8a6e350d-9a0e-4f2a-b51d-bbafbb436e81', 'A', 'misconception', 'wild type is always dominant'),
  ('8a6e350d-9a0e-4f2a-b51d-bbafbb436e81', 'C', 'misconception', 'wild type is the newest allele'),
  ('8a6e350d-9a0e-4f2a-b51d-bbafbb436e81', 'D', 'misconception', 'standard allele expressed only when homozygous');

-- [Sex-Linked Inheritance & Pedigrees] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Red-green color blindness is an X-linked recessive trait. In one family, a daughter is color blind, while her mother shows normal color vision. Based on the rules of X-linked recessive transmission, the daughter''s father must be:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "an affected male who passed a recessive-bearing \\(X\\) to the daughter"}, {"label": "B", "text": "an unaffected male who passed a recessive-bearing \\(X\\) to the daughter"}, {"label": "C", "text": "an affected male who passed his \\(Y\\) chromosome to the daughter"}, {"label": "D", "text": "an unaffected male who passed his \\(Y\\) chromosome to the daughter"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' For an X-linked recessive trait, a daughter is affected only when she is homozygous recessive (\(X^c X^c\)), which requires that she inherit a recessive allele from both parents; the textbook states that "females must inherit recessive X-linked alleles from both of their parents in order to express the trait." Because a father contributes his single \(X\) to every daughter (and his \(Y\) only to sons), and because a male is hemizygous—so "males need to inherit only one recessive mutant X allele to be affected"—any father whose \(X\) carries the recessive allele is himself color blind. (Choice A) is correct: the affected daughter''s \(X^c\) alleles must come one from each parent, so the father''s \(X\) carries the recessive allele, and a hemizygous male with that \(X\) is himself affected (\(X^c Y\)); the unaffected mother supplies the other \(X^c\) as a carrier. (Choice B) is incorrect: a male cannot silently carry an X-linked recessive allele, because he has only one \(X\); a father who transmits a recessive-bearing \(X\) is hemizygous \(X^c Y\) and therefore expresses the trait rather than being unaffected. (Choice C) is incorrect: a father passes his \(Y\) chromosome to his sons and his \(X\) chromosome to his daughters, so the \(Y\) never reaches a daughter and cannot be the route by which she receives an allele for this X-linked gene. (Choice D) is incorrect: it combines two errors—an unaffected father cannot be the source of a recessive \(X\) (he would be \(X^C Y\)), and the \(Y\) he does pass goes to sons, not daughters, so it cannot deliver the recessive allele the affected daughter needs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rules of X-linked recessive transmission—daughters inherit an \(X\) from each parent and males are hemizygous—to deduce a parent''s phenotype from an affected offspring.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '799571df-804e-47c0-827b-7c2f6a4fc172';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '799571df-804e-47c0-827b-7c2f6a4fc172';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('799571df-804e-47c0-827b-7c2f6a4fc172', 'B', 'misconception', 'male silently carries an X-linked recessive allele (ignores hemizygosity)'),
